WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.437
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.892 --> 00:00:08.160
SPEAKER: If you have
a dataset in BigQuery

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.040
that you wish to copy within
a region or from one region

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.310
to another, you can do so
without needing to extract,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.570
move, and reload the data.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.630
In this video, you
will learn two ways

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.080
to copy a BigQuery dataset
using the cloud console.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.530
Both options require
three preparation steps.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.810
First, review the
required permissions

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.180
in the documentation
page linked below

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.400
to ensure you have the
roles needed for the source

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.720
dataset, destination dataset,
and for creating transfers.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.720
Second, you must create
the destination dataset

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.820
where you would like
the copy to live.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.880
In this tutorial, we will
create the destination dataset

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.270
in the same project.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:48.510
However, this is not required.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.150
Third, you must enable
the BigQuery Data Transfer

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.480
Service in the same project
as the source dataset.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.100
From the Navigation menu, choose
APIs &amp; Services and Dashboard.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.130
Click to enable APIs &amp; Services.

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:06.430
Search for BigQuery
Data Transfer API,

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.440
then click Enable.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:13.160
Navigate back to BigQuery
using the search bar.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.440
Now, we are ready to
see the two options

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.180
for copying your dataset.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.330
Option one is using
the Copy Dataset icon.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.270
This method is simple, quick,
and perfect for a one time

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.620
dataset copy.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.690
Select the dataset
name of the source

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.580
dataset that you want to copy.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.430
Click the Copy Dataset icon.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.520
In the Copy Dataset dialog,
select the project ID

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.770
and destination dataset ID.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.040
Optionally, check the Overwrite
Destination Table box,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.140
if you want to
refresh or overwrite

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.940
the data and schema
of the tables

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.860
in the destination dataset.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.270
Click Copy.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.480
A Permissions window may appear
to give the BigQuery Data

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.350
Transfer Service permission
to manage your dataset copy.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.780
If so, click to allow.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.250
You can see the
progress and view

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:01.790
details of the dataset
copy under Transfers.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:03.530
Back in the BigQuery
console, you

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.660
will see the tables populate
under your destination dataset,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.660
once the transfer completes.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.669
Consider deleting
the old dataset

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:12.860
to avoid additional
storage costs,

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.270
if that makes sense
for your use case.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:19.140
Now, let's see option number
two, using the transfers UI.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.900
This option is best
if you want to copy

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:23.630
a dataset on a scheduled basis.

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.480
In the left-hand
nav, click Transfers.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.000
Click CREATE TRANSFER.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.700
In the source dropdown,
choose Dataset Copy.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.470
Give the transfer a name.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.270
In the Schedule
Options section, you

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.610
can choose when the
transfer will execute.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.200
And also, you may
set the transfer

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.300
to repeat at regular intervals.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.520
We will choose to start
the transfer immediately

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.800
and schedule it
to execute weekly.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.100
Now, choose your destination
dataset in the dropdown.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.950
You must copy in the name of
your source dataset and project

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:54.640
ID.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.020
Remember to use your project
ID, not your project name.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.910
Check the Overwrite
Destination Table

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:04.000
box, if you want to refresh all
data in the destination data

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.200
set.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.290
You can also receive email
notifications if and when

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:10.000
a transfer fails.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.960
Click Save.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.600
You can see progress and
view details of the dataset

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.920
in the transfers UI.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.540
Once the transfer
completes, you will

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.620
see the tables populate under
your destination data set.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.750
Back in the transfers
UI, make sure you

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.020
use the three dots to delete or
disable the recurring transfer,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.830
to avoid ongoing charges.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.450
There you have it--
two different ways

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.510
to copy data sets,
depending on your needs.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.660
At general availability,
data copied between regions

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.340
is billed at the
same rates as pricing

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.850
for compute engine network
egress between regions.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.950
BigQuery sends compressed data
for copying across regions,

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.100
so the gigabytes build
may actually be less

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.380
than the size of your dataset.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.510
All standard BigQuery
usage charges

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.750
for storage and querying will
apply on the copied data.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.480
More information on copying
datasets and pricing

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.580
is linked in the
description below.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:03.110
Happy analyzing.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

